===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|srew}}
From {{bor|en|xno|reume}}, from {{der|en|LL.|rheuma}}, from {{der|en|grc|ῥεῦμα|t=stream, humour}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ɹuːm/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|uːm|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Watery or thin discharge of [[serum]] or [[mucus]], especially from the [[eye]]s or [[nose]], formerly thought to cause disease. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant|I|iii|page=4|passage={{smallcaps|Shylock}}: You that did voide your '''rume''' vpon my beard, / And foote me as you ſpurne a ſtranger [[cur|curre]] / Ouer your threſhold, {{...}}}}
#* '''1599''', {{w|Thomas Nashe}}, ''Nashes Lenten Stuffe'':
#*: and there built ſutlers booths and tabernacles, to canopie their heads in from the '''rhewme''' of the heauens, or the clouds diſſoluing Cataracts.
#* '''1916''', {{w|James Joyce}}, ''A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man'', Macmillan Press Ltd, 102:
#*: He wore about his shoulders a heavy cloak; his pale face was drawn and his voice broken with '''rheum'''.
# [[illness|Illness]] or [[disease]] thought to be caused by such secretions; a [[cold]], [[catarrh]]; [[rheumatism]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Florio Montaigne Essayes|chapter=8|book=II|passage=he{{...}}had all his faculties free and easie, onely a '''rheume''' excepted that fell into his stomacke.}}
# {{lb|en|poetic}} [[tears|Tears]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|I|iv|27|2|''Rich.'' And ſay, what ſtore of parting tears were ſhed?<br>''Aum.'' Faith none for me: except the Northeaſt wind<br>Which then grew bitterly againſt our face,<br>Awak’d the ſleepie '''rhewme''', and ſo by chance<br>Did grace our hollow parting with a teare.}}

